    "result_text": "The suite has exactly one test class, and it passes. **BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required emitting an audit line on successful owner creation, via the logger named `AUDIT`, containing the owner id, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`.\n\nFollowing the codebase's established pattern \u2014 each business rule lives in its own small, static `Owner*Policy` class invoked from `addOwner` \u2014 I:\n\n1. **Added `service/OwnerAuditPolicy.java`** (new file): a tiny final class with a single static `auditCreate(Owner)` method (CC 1, no surrounding class complexity) that logs via `LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\")`.\n\n2. **Wired it into `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**: one call line right after `saveOwner(owner)` (so the id is populated), plus one import line.\n\n**Structural impact:** The only pre-existing function changed is `addOwner` (CC 1), with 1 changed line \u2014 the audit logic itself carries zero surrounding-class complexity because it sits in a fresh cohesive unit rather than inflating the large controller or service class. No existing method or class grew in complexity.\n\nThe AUDIT line is confirmed in the test run: `AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-02`, and the `audit-create` acceptance test (plus the full suite) is green.",
